REGISTRY/REPOSITORY:TAG.

29
00:01:58,633 --> 00:02:08,123
registry:3 runs a private registry; its HTTP API (/v2/…) shows repositories and tags.

30
00:02:09,867 --> 00:02:12,781
I keep hearing about Docker Hub.

31
00:02:12,781 --> 00:02:14,511
What is it, really?

32
00:02:15,067 --> 00:02:19,166
Docker Hub (docker.io) is Docker's default registry:

33
00:02:19,166 --> 00:02:23,976
every image name without a registry address comes from there.

34
00:02:23,976 --> 00:02:27,918
It hosts official images (nginx, python, postgres,

35
00:02:27,918 --> 00:02:36,511
maintained with Docker), images of verified publishers, and every user's and organisation's own repositories.

36
00:02:36,511 --> 00:02:44,001
To push, you log in with an access token (not your password); anonymous pulls are rate-limited.

37
00:02:45,767 --> 00:02:48,135
A short name is a Docker Hub name.

38
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:50,503
Both spellings are the same image:

39
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:56,006
Docker records where an image came from, with the digest of what it downloaded:

40
00:02:56,567 --> 00:03:02,143
Docker records where an image came from, with the digest of what it downloaded:

41
00:03:02,143 --> 00:03:04,402
A digest pins the exact content:

42
00:03:04,402 --> 00:03:08,003
a tag can be moved to a new image, a digest cannot.

43
00:03:08,003 --> 00:03:14,991
You can run an image by its digest (here it is already on your computer, so nothing is downloaded):

44
00:03:15,533 --> 00:03:17,797
